        answer: 0,
        explanation: `<p><strong>Celiac disease is associated with HLA-DQ2 and DQ8.</strong> These HLA molecules on antigen-presenting cells bind gliadin peptides from gluten especially well and present them to T helper cells. The T cells then activate B cells, which make the IgA antibodies used in diagnosis (tTG, DGP, and EMA), and the immune response damages the villi.</p>
<p>Almost everyone with celiac disease carries DQ2 or DQ8, so a negative HLA test makes celiac disease very unlikely; a positive one alone does not diagnose it, because the types are common.</p>`,
