        stem: "Which findings on duodenal biopsy confirm celiac disease?",
        options: ["Villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytes", "PAS-positive foamy macrophages packing the lamina propria of the villi", "Noncaseating granulomas with transmural inflammation", "Enterocytes filled with fat droplets and normal villi", "Crypt abscesses limited to the mucosa"],
        answer: 0,
        explanation: `<p><strong>Celiac disease shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytes.</strong> T cells reacting to gliadin destroy enterocytes at the villus tips, so the villi flatten. The crypts deepen as they try to replace the lost cells, and the lymphocytes sit within the epithelium.</p>
<p>Flat villi mean less surface for absorption, which is why celiac disease is a true malabsorption. Biopsies are taken from the bulb (1–2) and the distal duodenum (4), because the damage can be patchy.</p>`,
