        explanation: `<p><strong>Kayser-Fleischer rings are copper deposited at the rim of the cornea</strong> (in Descemet membrane), and they mark Wilson disease. When ATP7B fails, copper cannot leave the liver in bile; it builds up in hepatocytes and spills into the blood as free copper, reaching the brain, cornea, kidneys, and RBCs.</p>
<p>A tremor fits the same process: copper in the basal ganglia (putamen) causes tremor, parkinsonism, and psychiatric symptoms. The slit-lamp exam is part of the workup, along with ceruloplasmin and 24-hour urine copper. A sunflower cataract is the lens's version of the same deposit.</p>`,
