        condition: "liver-tests",
        kind: "reasoning",
        stem: "A 20-year-old student has yellow eyes after 2 days of fasting during gastroenteritis. Total bilirubin is 48 µmol/L, direct 4 µmol/L, and AST, ALT, ALP, FBC, and blood film are normal. Which management is most appropriate?",
        options: ["Reassurance with no treatment", "MRCP to look for a bile duct stone", "Phototherapy", "Ursodeoxycholic acid", "Liver biopsy"],
        answer: 0,
        explanation: `<p><strong>An isolated unconjugated rise after fasting or illness, with normal enzymes and FBC, is Gilbert syndrome,</strong> which needs no treatment. UGT1A1 works at 30–50% of normal, enough on an ordinary day; fasting, illness, menstruation, or exertion tip the balance, and unconjugated bilirubin builds up until the trigger passes.</p>
<p>The normal FBC and film exclude hemolysis, the other cause of an unconjugated rise, and normal enzymes show there is no liver injury, so imaging and biopsy add nothing.</p>`,
