        stem: "Two patients have a bilirubin of 70 µmol/L. One has hereditary spherocytosis during a viral illness; the other has a stone completely blocking the common bile duct. How would their urine urobilinogen differ?",
        options: ["Increased in spherocytosis, absent with the stone", "Absent in spherocytosis, increased with the stone", "Normal in both, since urobilinogen reflects kidney function", "Increased in both, since both raise serum bilirubin", "Absent in both, since neither patient's bilirubin is conjugated"],
        answer: 0,
        explanation: `<p><strong>Urobilinogen is made in the gut, so it tracks how much bilirubin reaches the gut.</strong> Gut bacteria turn conjugated bilirubin into urobilinogen; 10–20% is reabsorbed, and some reaches the urine.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hemolysis:</strong> more heme means more bilirubin made, conjugated, and delivered to the gut, so more urobilinogen forms and more reaches the urine.</li>
<li><strong>Complete obstruction:</strong> no bilirubin reaches the gut, so none forms and urine urobilinogen is absent.</li>
</ul>
<p>The obstructed patient's urine is still dark, from conjugated bilirubin itself.</p>`,
